><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span><span><strong>No law</strong><span class="t">makes a South Carolina HOA hand a buyer its papers</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>2 in 3</strong><span class="t">Horry County owners who complained to the state got the papers after buying</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated July 27, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">South Carolina gives a buyer no HOA packet by law. The red flags, the papers to ask for, and when to read them.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Look for six red flags. The first is a rule that blocks your plan. The other five put your money or loan at risk: thin savings, big repairs, unpaid dues, a lawsuit, and too much commercial space or one big owner. The rules, the budget, the board's minutes and the lender's condo questionnaire show them.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina law does not make a homeowners association (HOA) hand a buyer these papers, and gives no right to cancel over them. The review time in your contract is when to read them.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Send the address. We read the HOA papers before your review time ends.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us read the HOA papers</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#red-flags" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Red flags</a></li><li><a href="#the-documents" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The documents</a></li><li><a href="#sc-law" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What SC law gives you</a></li><li><a href="#public-record" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Public record</a></li><li><a href="#rules-change" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Rules change</a></li><li><a href="#when-they-arrive" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">When they arrive</a></li><li><a href="#too-late" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0