s (animation-timeline:view()){.c3ph-rowbox{timeline-scope:--c3ph-p1,--c3ph-p2,--c3ph-p3}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(1){view-timeline:--c3ph-p1 inline}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(2){view-timeline:--c3ph-p2 inline}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(3){view-timeline:--c3ph-p3 inline}.c3ph-dots i{animation:c3ph-dot linear both;animation-range:cover}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(1){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p1}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(2){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p2}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(3){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p3}@keyframes c3ph-dot{0%,36%{background:transparent}42%,60%{background:var(--navy)}66%,100%{background:transparent}}}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-swipe{display:none}}.c3ph-cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.75rem;margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;padding:0;list-style:none}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-cards{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem}}@media (min-width:1000px){.c3ph-cards.c3ph-4{grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr))}}.c3ph-card{margin:0;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.c3ph-card .c3ph-f{border-radius:0}.c3ph-card img,.c3ph-card svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3);object-fit:cover}.c3ph-card .c3ph-cr{font-size:.56rem;padding:1.2rem .45rem .25rem 1.6rem}.c3ph-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;padding:.7rem .8rem 0}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:760px}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Six things in the papers should stop you. A rule that blocks how you plan to use the home comes first, because a lower price does not change it. The other five are tests that decide whether a building passes a loan review.</p><ul class="hd-flags" aria-label="Red flags in HOA documents, where each shows up and the test"><li class="hot"><span class="k">In the declaration, master deed and rules</span><b>A rule that blocks your plan</b>A limit on renting, pets, parking, boats or golf carts that rules out how you will use the home. In a condo, the master deed must state any limit on leasing, such as how long a lease must run.<span class="w">More on <a href="/hoa/rental-restrictions/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental rules</a></span></li><li><span class="k">In the budget</span><b>Thin savings</b>In Fannie Mae's full review of a condo building, the budget must put at least 10 percent of dues income into savings for big repairs, called reserves. Freddie Mac raises its test to 15 percent for loan applications from January 4, 2027.<span class="w">How we read <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA reserves</a></span></li><li><span class="k">In the minutes and the reserve study</span><b>Big repairs waiting</b>Fannie Mae will not approve a building that needs critical repairs or has significant deferred maintenance, meaning major upkeep put off.<span class="w">What a <a href="/hoa/special-assessments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">special assessment</a> can cost</span></li><li><span class="k">In the lender's condo questionnaire</span><b>Too many owners behind on dues</b>In Fannie Mae's full review, no more than 15 percent of units may be 60 days or more behind on dues. In a 100-unit building, that is 15 units.<span class="w">The building can fail the loan review</span></li><li><span class="k">In the minutes and the questionnaire</span><b>A lawsuit about the building</b>A suit naming the association can block a normal loan. So can a suit against the builder about safety, structure, use or whether units can be lived in.<span class="w">Buying a <a href="/buyers/condo-in-litigation/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condo in litigation</a></span></li><li><span class="k">In the questionnaire</span><b>Too much commercial space, or one big owner</b>Fannie Mae caps shops and other commercial space at 35 percent of the building. In a building of 21 or more units, one owner may hold no more than 20 percent of them.<span class="w">The building can fail the loan review</span></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Fannie Mae buys home loans from lenders. A building that fails the review is non-warrantable: a loan on a unit there cannot be sold to Fannie Mae, so buyers need a different loan, often at a higher cost. Buildings with a rental program here are usually non-warrantable because so many owners are investors.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our agents read the rules against how you plan to live: a boat, a golf cart, a rental, a pool. Many communities ban renting a house out at all. When we search for a buyer who plans to rent, we filter those out first, then read the HOA papers for any rule that would undo the plan.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Will a lender approve the building?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send the building's name. We read its budget and minutes against the five lender tests above and tell you which ones it fails.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us test the building</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="the-documents"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The documents">Which HOA documents should you get before buying?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Ten papers, from four places: the county, the association, the seller and your lender. Most buyers here need them. In our listing search, nearly every condo, 9 of 10 townhouses and about 7 of 10 houses for sale list an association fee.</p><figure class="hd-fig hd-docfig"><p class="cap" id="hd-docs-cap">The HOA documents to get, and what each one tells you</p><table class="hd-docs" aria-labelledby="hd-docs-cap"><thead><tr><th scope="col">The document</th><th scope="col">Where it comes from</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Declaration, or master deed for a condo<small>Also called the CC&amp;Rs: covenants, conditions and restrictions</small></th><td data-label="Where it comes from">Recorded at the county</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What you own, what is shared, your share of the costs and any limits on renting</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Bylaws</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">Recorded at the county</td><td data-label="What it tells you">How the board is elected, how votes work and how the rules get changed</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Rules and regulations</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association; filed at the county each January</td><td data-label="What it tells you">The day-to-day rules: pets, parking, the pool, trash, what you can build</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Budget, balance sheet and income statement</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association or its manager</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What comes in, what goes out and how much is saved for big repairs</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Reserve study</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association or its manager</td><td data-label="What it tells you">When the roof, paving and other big parts need replacing, and whether the savings cover it</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Meeting minutes, 12 to 24 months</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association or its manager</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What the board plans next: repairs, a special assessment, rule changes, a lawsuit</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Master insurance certificate</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association or its manager</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What the building's policy covers and what you insure yourself. See <a href="/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">master and HO-6 insurance</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Statement of amounts due<small>Also called an estoppel letter</small></th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The association, before closing</td><td data-label="What it tells you">What the seller still owes the association. See <a href="/hoa/estoppel-and-transfer-fees/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">estoppel and transfer fees</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Condo questionnaire</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">Your lender sends it to the association</td><td data-label="What it tells you">Owners behind on dues, lawsuits, commercial space: the answers a lender needs</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Seller's disclosure statement, with its HOA addendum</th><td data-label="Where it comes from">The seller, before the contract</td><td data-label="What it tells you">The seller's yes-or-no answers to 11 questions about the association</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina law calls the declaration, the master deed and the bylaws the governing documents. In a condo, which the law calls a horizontal property regime, the master deed is the paper that matters most. The law says what it must contain:</p><figure class="hd-fig"><p class="cap">What South Carolina's condo law puts in a master deed</p><div class="hd-md"><div class="hd-page" role="img" aria-label="A drawing of a master deed page with five marked parts, numbered 1 to 5"><span class="t">MASTER DEED</span><span class="ln"></span><span class="ln s"></span><span class="mk"><i>1</i><span class="ln"></span></span><span class="ln"></span><span class="ln s"></span><span class="mk"><i>2</i><span class="ln"></span></span><span class="ln"></span><span class="ln s"></span><span class="mk"><i>3</i><span class="ln"></span></span><span class="ln"></span><span class="ln s"></span><span class="mk"><i>4</i><span class="ln"></span></span><span class="ln"></span><span class="ln s"></span><span class="mk"><i>5</i><span class="ln"></span></span><span class="ln s"></span></div><ol class="hd-mdkey"><li><b>Your unit's share of the costs</b>The percentage of the shared expenses each unit pays.</li><li><b>What is shared</b>The common elements, and any shared areas that belong to certain units only.</li><li><b>Any limit on renting</b>Limits on leasing a unit, including the amount and the length of a lease.</li><li><b>The bylaws</b>Recorded with the master deed, so they are on file at the county too.</li><li><b>Changing how it is run</b>Owners holding two-thirds of the condo's value must agree, in a recorded paper.</li></ol></div><figcaption>Our drawing, from South Carolina's condo law. It shows what the law requires, not any one condo's text.</figcaption></figure><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">The recorded papers for one address</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send the address. We pull the declaration or master deed and the bylaws from the county and mark the rules on renting, pets and parking.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us pull the declaration</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="sc-law"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What SC law gives you">Does an HOA have to give a buyer its documents in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. No South Carolina law makes an association give a buyer a resale packet, gives it a deadline, or caps its fee. The state's <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c030.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HOA law</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">condo law</a> and <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t33c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nonprofit law</a> have no such rule.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller's duty is a form. Unless the sale is exempt, the seller fills in the state's <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Property-Condition-Disclosure-Statement-06.2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">disclosure form</a> before the contract is signed. If the home is in an association, that includes a one-page HOA addendum.</p><div class="hd-two"><div><p class="k">The seller's form asks yes or no</p><ul><li>Dues and other charges</li><li>Limits on reselling or renting</li><li>Special assessments</li><li>Limits on guests and pets</li><li>Assigned parking, keys and fobs</li><li>Other memberships that come with the home</li><li>Known problems in shared areas</li><li>The state's coastal zone law</li><li>A transfer fee</li></ul></div><div class="only"><p class="k">Only the papers show</p><ul><li>The exact words of each rule</li><li>How much the association has saved</li><li>What the board plans next</li><li>What the seller still owes</li><li>Whether a lender will approve the building</li></ul></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller may answer any question "No Representation", which means no answer at all. The form itself tells buyers to read the documents before signing a contract. If the seller never gives the form, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the law says</a> the contract still stands and the closing goes ahead.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Some sales skip the form, for example the first sale of a new home no one has lived in, a foreclosure sale, an auction, or a sale where both sides agree in writing to skip it.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What other states give a buyer</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Buyers who move here from Virginia, Maryland or Florida often expect the papers to arrive by law, with days to cancel. Those three are among the states that send the most movers to Horry County.</p><ul class="hd-states" aria-label="What the law gives a buyer of a home in an association, by state"><li class="sc"><b>South Carolina</b><span class="k">The law hands the buyer</span>No HOA packet. Unless the sale is exempt, the seller's disclosure form, with a one-page HOA addendum, before the contract.<span class="k">The way out</span>No right to cancel tied to the HOA papers. Only what your contract gives you.</li><li><b>Virginia</b><span class="k">The law hands the buyer</span>The association's resale certificate. Unless the sale is exempt, the seller must get it for the buyer.<span class="k">The way out</span>If the contract names no period, 3 days after the buyer gets it. Any time before settlement if it never comes.</li><li><b>Maryland</b><span class="k">The law hands the buyer</span>The HOA disclosures, within 20 calendar days of the contract. The law caps the association's fee for them.<span class="k">The way out</span>5 calendar days to cancel if they come late.</li><li><b>Florida, in an HOA</b><span class="k">The law hands the buyer</span>A disclosure summary before the buyer signs.<span class="k">The way out</span>If it came late: void the contract within 3 days of getting it or before closing, whichever comes first.</li><li><b>North Carolina, in a condo</b><span class="k">The law hands the buyer</span>In most condo resales, a statement of the monthly dues and other fees, before closing.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A bill filed in February 2026, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/sess126_2025-2026/bills/5204.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">H. 5204</a>, would require an HOA seller packet on a website at no charge. It would also require the seller certification and governing documents within 15 days of the seller's request. It has not moved out of committee and is not law.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="public-record"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Public record">Are HOA documents public record in Horry County?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, the recorded ones. An association's declaration or master deed, its bylaws and their amendments must be filed with the county's <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/register-of-deeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Register of Deeds</a> to be enforced. Rules stay enforceable only if filed there by January 10 of the year after they are adopted or changed.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County's <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">index</a> of recorded papers is online. The pages themselves are not, so we read and copy them at the county for you.</p><div class="hd-county"><p class="k">Horry County Register of Deeds</p><ul><li><strong>1984</strong><span>The online index finds every paper under a name back to this year. The pages are not online.</span></li><li><strong>$64</strong><span>A copy of a 60-page declaration: $8 for the first 4 pages and $1 for each page after. A certified copy is $2 more.</span></li><li><strong>Conway</strong><span>The public viewing area is at 1301 Second Avenue, open Monday to Friday, 8 a.m. to 4:45 p.m. Copies also come by mail.</span></li></ul></div><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/horry-government-justice-center-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="675" alt="The Horry County Government and Justice Center in Conway: brick wings on each side of a white columned entrance, seen across the parking lot" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia. License: CC BY-SA 3.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 3.0">Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia</a></span></div><figcaption>Horry County's Government and Justice Center in Conway. The Register of Deeds is here, with a public viewing area where recorded HOA papers can be read and printed.</figcaption></figure></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The papers the county does not keep</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The budget, the minutes, the reserve study and the insurance certificate come from the association or its manager. The right to see them belongs to owners, so the seller, who still owns the home, can ask for them.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">An owner of a nonprofit association can inspect and copy its articles, bylaws, minutes of owners' meetings and three years of financial statements. The owner gives <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t33c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">written notice</a> five business days ahead and pays only the cost of copies. If the association is not a nonprofit corporation, the right covers its yearly budget and owner list.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a condo, the board must keep a dated book of every receipt and expense. It is open to all owners at set hours on working days.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">The papers the county does not keep</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">The budget, the minutes and the reserve study come from the association. We ask the seller and the manager for them the day you sign.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask us to get the budget and minutes</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="rules-change"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Rules change">How often do HOA rules change in Horry County?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Often. In the 12 months to October 2026, about 700 association papers were recorded in Horry County. More than 300 associations filed them, and about 400 were rules and policies.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">By title, 60 were fine policies, 43 were collection policies for unpaid dues, and 83 were architectural or design rules.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Before 2018, almost no rules were on file. A 2018 law made an association's papers enforceable only once recorded. Associations that already existed had until January 10, 2019, and 554 papers were filed that January alone.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Association papers filed at the Horry County Register of Deeds, by year"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Association papers filed at the Horry County Register of Deeds, by year</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Year, what happened</span><span role="columnheader">papers filed</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2012</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.034" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">29</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2013</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.052" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">45</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2014</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.053" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">46</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2015</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.053" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">46</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2016</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.057" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">49</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2017</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.066" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">57</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2018</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">the HOA law took effect in May</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.395" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">340</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">2019</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">554 in January alone</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">861</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">The last 12 months</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">about 400 of them rules</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.813" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 700</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Our count of the county's index, papers filed as restrictions. From 2012 to 2017 only 1 of them was a set of rules. The last 12 months run from November 2025 to October 2026.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The rules you read under contract can change after you buy. A rule takes effect when the board adopts it, and it has until the next January 10 to reach the county. Until then, only the association's own copy shows it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">An association must give an owner a copy of its rules on request, unless they are posted in a common area or on its website. The minutes show what the board plans next, and we read them for that.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="when-they-arrive"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="When they arrive">When do you get HOA documents, and what if you find a problem?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller's form comes before the contract. Everything else arrives during your review time, if someone asks for it. We ask the day you sign.</p><ol class="hd-steps" aria-label="When the HOA papers arrive, in order"><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before you offer</span><b>The seller's form, and the recorded papers</b>Unless the sale is exempt, the seller fills in the state's disclosure form before the contract is signed. We pull the recorded declaration or master deed from the county.</span></li><li class="hot"><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span class="c"><span class="t">When you sign</span><b>Your review time starts</b>No South Carolina law gives you a set number of days for the HOA papers. The review time in your contract, often called the due diligence period, is your clock.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span class="c"><span class="t">During the review</span><b>The papers the association keeps</b>We ask for the rules, budget, minutes, reserve study and insurance certificate right away. Owners must give five business days' written notice, so the request goes in early. Your lender sends the condo questionnaire.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before closing</span><b>What the seller still owes</b>The association's statement shows any unpaid dues. A condo buyer, other than a buyer at a foreclosure sale, owes the seller's unpaid dues along with the seller. The buyer owes no more than the statement says.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span class="c"><span class="t">After you buy</span><b>The rules keep changing</b>New rules take effect when the board adopts them and are filed at the county each January. As an owner, you can now ask to see the books yourself.</span></li></ol><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">If the papers show a problem</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most problems have one of three answers.</p><ul class="hd-flags hd-fix" aria-label="What to do if the papers show a problem"><li><span class="k">Answer 1</span><b>The association explains it</b>We ask the manager in writing what the line in the budget or minutes means, and how much any coming bill will be.</li><li><span class="k">Answer 2</span><b>The seller pays or fixes it</b>We ask the seller to pay what is owed, or to take a coming bill off the price.</li><li class="hot"><span class="k">Answer 3</span><b>You walk away in time</b>If the problem is a rule that blocks your plan, the review time in your contract is when to leave, on the terms your contract gives.</li></ul><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Under contract already?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send the address and the last day of your review time. We get the papers, read them first and call you with what we found.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send us your review deadline</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="too-late"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Too late">What happens to buyers who read HOA documents too late?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many learn the rules after it is too late to walk away. Of Horry County owners who complained to the state about an HOA from 2022 through 2025, about two in three got the papers after they bought.</p><figure class="hd-fig"><p class="cap">Horry County owners who complained to the state about an HOA</p><p class="hd-q">When did they get the HOA papers?</p><div class="hd-stack" role="img" aria-label="Of 359 who answered, 124 got the papers before buying and 235 after buying"><i class="a" style="width:34.5%"><span>35%</span></i><i class="c" style="width:65.5%"><span>65%</span></i></div><ul class="hd-key"><li><i class="a" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>124</b> before they bought</span></li><li><i class="c" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>235</b> after they bought</span></li></ul><p class="hd-q">When were they told the home was in an HOA?</p><div class="hd-stack" role="img" aria-label="Of 372 who answered, 198 were told before closing, 119 at closing and 55 after closing"><i class="a" style="width:53.2%"><span>53%</span></i><i class="b" style="width:32.0%"><span>32%</span></i><i class="c" style="width:14.8%"><span>15%</span></i></div><ul class="hd-key"><li><i class="a" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>198</b> before closing</span></li><li><i class="b" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>119</b> at closing</span></li><li><i class="c" aria-hidden="true"></i><span><b>55</b> after closing</span></li></ul><figcaption>Complaints to SC Consumer Affairs from 2022 through 2025, counting only those who answered each question. These are owners who complained, not all owners.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Of the 413 who answered, 55 said they had been denied access to the governing documents, and 49 said they had not received a copy.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More of the complaints in the <a href="https://www.consumer.sc.gov/HOA-reports" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">state's 2026 HOA report</a> came from Horry County than from any other county: 31 percent of 452.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The fix is timing. We get the papers in front of you while the review time is still open, and we read them for you first. We tell you what the rules, the money and the lender's tests mean for you before you decide.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="hoa-documents-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">HOA documents FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Are HOA documents public record in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The recorded ones are: the declaration or master deed, the bylaws, their amendments and the rules. In Horry County the index is online and the pages are read in Conway or ordered by mail. The budget, minutes and reserve study come from the association.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I back out of buying a house because of the HOA documents?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not under a South Carolina law, which gives no right to cancel tied to the HOA papers. Your contract's review time is when to act. Virginia, Maryland and Florida law each give a buyer a set number of days.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long does it take to get HOA documents?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No South Carolina law gives an association a deadline to send them. We ask the manager for the papers, the fee and the date the day you sign.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does the seller have to tell me there is an HOA?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, unless the sale is exempt, such as the first sale of a new home. The seller's disclosure form asks, and a yes brings the one-page HOA addendum. A missing form does not undo the contract.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does CCRs mean in an HOA?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Covenants, conditions and restrictions, also written CC and Rs: the rules recorded with the declaration that come with the home. In a condo, the same rules are in the master deed.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c030.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-30</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-31</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t33c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 33-31</a>, <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Property-Condition-Disclosure-Statement-06.2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC disclosure form</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/t3wpam13/customer-service-request-form.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County Register of Deeds</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deed index</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.2-02/full-review-process" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.2-02</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>, <a href="https://guide.freddiemac.com/app/guide/bulletin/2026-C" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac Bulletin 2026-C</a>, <a href="https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter23.1/section55.1-2312/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Code of Virginia 55.1-2312</a>, <a href="https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Laws/StatuteText?article=grp&section=11B-106" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Maryland 11B-106</a>, <a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Laws/Statutes/2026/720.401" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Florida 720.401</a>, <a href="https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_47C/GS_47C-4-109.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NC 47C-4-109</a>, <a href="https://www.consumer.sc.gov/HOA-reports" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDCA HOA reports</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/sess126_2025-2026/bills/5204.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC H. 5204</a>. Our count of Horry County's deed index and of the state's complaint workbooks. Listings: our listing search, in aggregate.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We are not attorneys, and nothing on this page is legal advice. For advice on your own contract, talk to a South Carolina attorney.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Horry County's Government and Justice Center in Conway. Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped and resized the photo.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/hoa-documents/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Horry County's Government and Justice Center in Conway","caption":"Horry County's Government and Justice Center in Conway. The Register of Deeds is here, with a public viewing area where recorded HOA papers can be read and printed.","description":"The Horry County Government and Justice Center in Conway: brick wings on each side of a white columned entrance, seen across the parking lot","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Pollinator at English Wikipedia"},"creditText":"Pollinator at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"© Pollinator at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"Horry County Government and Justice Center, 1301 Second Avenue, Conway, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Get the HOA papers read before you are committed.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Send the community name. We get the papers, read them and tell you what we found.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us get and read the papers</a></div></section></main>
